A||N||7||Monkeyman||Oops!  Coolant line boo-boo.||||1||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||11-17-2001||04:28 PM||||I just installed my amp with self drilling screws to the rear firewall being careful not to drive the screws into anything in the engine compartment.  Oops.  Now I have a new hole in one of my coolant tubes.  <P>This is on the passenger side.  Can I get to these from underneath the car without removing a wheel/liner?  Are these hoses reasonably priced?  (I can remember radiator hoses for "generic" cars running less than 20 bucks.)  Is there anything else I may have drilled/screwed into?  (It's definitely coolant as I now have a nice green puddle under my car.)<P>Any info would be appreciated.  (Anyone gonna be in my area tomorrow?)<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/patriot.gif">    <BR><B><FONT COLOR="red">Proud</FONT> <FONT COLOR="white">to be an</FONT> <FONT COLOR="blue">American</FONT>!!</B>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||11-17-2001||07:25 PM||||::bump::<P>Help, guys.  I need to fix this tomorrow so I can drive it to work on Monday.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/patriot.gif">    <BR><B><FONT COLOR="red">Proud</FONT> <FONT COLOR="white">to be an</FONT> <FONT COLOR="blue">American</FONT>!!</B>||||reg||1
Z||000002||tgowens||11-17-2001||09:44 PM||||MM, the coolant tube runs from the radiator up front, underneath the car and up to the water pump hose in the rear. it is all one piece stainless steel. It is fairly easy to remove - it is held to the bottom of the car with two brackets.<P>Yes, you can get to it without removing the wheel liner as in the rear it turns up into the engine bay right in front of the wheel liner. There are two hoses connectint to the rear of the tube while only one connects in the front......<P>Now, for the bad news........ these are not "generic" - they are Fiero specific. I know I have some here but, since I am in CA I would imagine that there are a lot of people with coolant tubes much closer to you than I am...<P>You could, however, for a temporary fix, wrap a section of rubber tubing around the area with the hole and clamp tightly on both ends -- I still would replace the tube though unless you can do a stainless steel repair to the affected area.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_TGO.jpg">  <P><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A><p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 11-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||RossT||11-17-2001||10:00 PM||||I have had good luck with coolants leaks using  "epoxy puddy". I once had a hole in my caravans radiator.  Put a small dab of epoxy puddy on it and it held for 5 years, then I sold the van.  I would use the epoxy and tgowens fix until you can get another part.<P>Epoxy puddy will work wet. 10 years ago I had a crack in a toilet.  Stuck my hand thru the water and sealed the leak.  Its still holding today.<P>If you buy some, get the stuff that says it will work on automotive cooling systems.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||11-18-2001||12:16 PM||||Well, it's not the front to rear coolant tube.  I tried to get under the car to see everything else better, but couldn't get under there far enough.  There's coolant dripping from just about everywhere on the passenger side rear (towards the front of the cradle).  I moved the rubber hose that attaches to the rear of the coolant tube (couldn't see more than a few inches of it) and it seemed to move fine (not like a screw was still embedded in it).  I guess I'll fill it up with water and ease it down to the dealer.  Hopefully they'll be able to work on it tomorrow.  Maybe I'll just trade it in for a Supra.||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||11-18-2001||12:26 PM||||There is an aluminum tube running the length of the car for the heater. It comes up the firewall and goes around the passenger side of the engine compartment to the rear of the engine near the alternator. It is clamped to the firewall behind the seats, and lays in the right frame rail. you can find the hole and cut the tube and splice with a piece of rubber 5/8 heater hose. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||8Ball||11-18-2001||04:17 PM||||I have replaced my coolant hoses on my motor with generic hoses as I could not get a direct replacement at the time. And it works just fine for me.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/8bff.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||11-18-2001||05:48 PM||||For a small, clean, hole like that....<P>in matal tube, clamp a patch over it. You need a quarter sized scrap of rubber and a standard hose clamp. Center the patch and clamp over the hole... If the hole is more than 1/8-3/16, put a piece of metal over the rubber and then clamp it. This is a pretty permanent fix. At the least this will get you by untill you replace the metal. This is a standard method of plumbing repair. Works best if the rubber is cut from a scrap of heater/coolant hose. But even a double thickness of inner tube will work.<P>in rubber line, just get a line splice and clamps at any parts store, K/wall mart, etc. Cut the line at the hole and install the splice. Fix is permanent if the rest of the line is good. This will work for any hole that isn't right at the end of the line or in a tight curve. If it's in a curve you'll need to get an elbow fitting from a  home store and clamp that in. You want the kind of elbow used for flexible tubing.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
